Drives its own private GC state (`state = []i64(gcWords)`, bound via
`gcBindMemory`/`gcInit` to its own arena) and its own collection driver
(`chanDriveCollection`, called explicitly twice), not the ambient runtime
collector — the two `print`ed scans assert on THAT collector's own counts
(`gcNumObjects`/`gcStatTotalSwept`), which are correct regardless of whether
the ambient (libbitrt) collector ever runs at all. Same shape as
_tests_/stress/chanmakeroots, which builds the channel through `chan_make`
instead of by hand.

CORRECTION: "the ambient collector has nothing live to find and legitimately
never fires under BIT_GC=stress" was false. Measured, aarch64-macos, 5 runs
each policy: default `collections=0` every time, `BIT_GC=stress`
`collections=113` every time (denominator: 5/5, zero spread) — matching
stress.bit's own documented scheduler-loop floor
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`). Confirmed default and
`BIT_GC=stress` stdout are byte-identical to `.expected`
(`scan1 6 0 6 6015` / `scan2 0 6` / `collections 2`).

Every allocation this program makes goes through the private `g` handle, so
the ambient collector never traces or sweeps any of its objects. The backing
arenas are ordinary managed `[]i64` slices, though, and this program's own
header used to claim they are "held live in main for the whole program" —
that was lexical-scope reasoning and is not what this compiler's precise,
interval-based stack maps actually protect (#4097 found the gap for real in
gcbit.bit: it segfaulted under BIT_GC=stress without an explicit trailing
read). #4184 RESOLVED: measured this file's own compiled stack maps directly
and found its current codegen keeps every one of these bindings precisely
rooted through virtually the whole function anyway (an accident of
conservative interval widening, not a documented guarantee — see main()'s
own leading comment). The `rooted` field in the final print, mirroring
gcbit's `alive` and gcspanblocks'/gcspanfree's/gcspanfreelist's own
`rooted`, closes the gap for real regardless of whether that accident
continues to hold across future compiler changes.
